{
	"info": {
		"name": "CCCBR Orbitas Propiedades",
		"description": "Tests for GET /wp-json/cccbr-orbitas/v1/propiedades.\n\n## Before you run it\n\nThis collection contains **no credentials**. Create a Postman *environment* (never committed — `postman/*.postman_environment.json` is git-ignored) with these variables:\n\n| Variable | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `base_url` | e.g. `http://localhost:8080` or `https://camara.cr` — no trailing slash |\n| `username` | a WordPress user holding the `cccbr_orbitas_read_propiedades` capability |\n| `app_password` | that user's **Application Password**, spaces and all |\n| `noaccess_username` | a user **without** the capability, for the 403 test |\n| `noaccess_app_password` | that user's Application Password |\n\nThe collection authenticates with HTTP Basic at collection level; the 401 request switches auth off and the 403 request overrides the credentials.\n\n## How to run it\n\nUse the Collection Runner (or `newman run`) so the folders execute in order: *Full walk* loops over itself through the whole collection using the keyset cursor, and *Consistency* depends on values the earlier requests store.\n\n## The manual case: a record added during a walk\n\nOne acceptance case cannot be automated from Postman alone, because it needs a property to be created while the walk is in progress:\n\n1. Run *Full walk* and note `totals.total` from the first page.\n2. While it is running (or between two pages), create a property in WordPress.\n3. Finish the walk.\n\nExpected: the new property has a **higher id** than every page already fetched, so it appears at the end of the walk and **never duplicates** an earlier row — that is the guarantee keyset pagination gives and offset pagination does not. The walk's item count may exceed the first page's `totals.total` by exactly the number of properties added. To pin the set to the moment the walk started, record `pagination.max_id` from the first page and ignore any id above it.",
		"schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
	},
